    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bromide was originally called Juanita and then Zenobia before taking its current name from the bromine in the local mineral water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        24,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromide%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The townsite of Bromide was part of the Chickasaw Nation before Oklahoma achieved statehood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromide%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bromide was founded by Judge William H. and incorporated in July 1908.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        9,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=BR021"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Abandoned limestone buildings, including a three-story hotel and bathhouse, remain as a testament to the prosperous past of Bromide.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/bromide-oklahoma-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Limestone quarries in Bromide supplied stone for several historic courthouses and cathedrals across the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/bromide-oklahoma-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
